---
import { SitePage } from '@boombapbot/editor';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="About · boombapbot"
  description="boombapbot is a drum machine that runs in your browser: what it is, how it is made, and what it keeps."
>
  <SitePage client:load baseUrl={base} current="about">
    <h1>About boombapbot</h1>
    <p class="sb-lede">
      boombapbot is a drum machine that runs in your browser. There is nothing to install, you don't need an
      account, and what you play stays on your computer.
    </p>

    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>
      The page is one machine, РИТМ-12. I made it up: a rhythm computer from a factory that never existed,
      lettered in Russian. It has eight instruments, sixteen step keys, four patterns and one effect. You pick
      an instrument, press the steps it should play, and press play.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href={`${base}docs`}>docs</a> go through every control and say what the lettering means. Or just
      open <a href={base}>the machine</a> and press play.
    </p>

    <h2>How it is made</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The sounds are synthesised.</strong> Each one is built from its knob settings as you turn them.
        Nothing is a recording, and none of them copies a real machine. I made them by ear.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One engine keeps the time.</strong> The sequencer and the sounds are a single program that runs
        on the browser's audio thread. Every step is placed on the audio clock, so the beat holds steady whatever
        else the page is doing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The panel is a picture.</strong> It is my own artwork, and every key, knob and switch in it is
        live. The screen and the meter are drawn by the page while it plays.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What it keeps, and where</h2>
    <p>
      Your patterns and knob positions are saved in this browser every time you change something, and they are
      there when you come back. They are not sent anywhere unless you save a beat to an account. To keep a copy
      or move to another device, export the project file from settings, or save the beat to a free account and
      load it there; you can also export audio and MIDI.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site loads its own files and nothing else. There are no analytics and no ads. Making an account is
      optional, and logging in sets the site's one cookie.
    </p>

    <h2>What came before</h2>
    <p>
      boombapbot started out as a different machine. If you made a beat on it, it opens here as a РИТМ-12 beat,
      and the page lists what did not come across: that machine had more instruments and more rhythms than this
      one has room for. A beat you saved to an account back then is still there as it was.
    </p>

    <h2>What is not here yet</h2>
    <p>Upgrades. That button in the footer only says so for now.</p>

    <h2>Who made it</h2>
    <p>
      One person. I have a master's in software engineering, I work in QA, I'm autistic, and I love writing code
      about as much as I love electronic music. boombapbot is what happens when those overlap for long enough. I
      build it in my spare time, on a little too much coffee and a little too much stress.
    </p>
    <p>
      It's an independent project. РИТМ-12 and the maker's name on its panel are invented, and it copies no
      real machine. The <a href={`${base}policies`}>policies</a> page has the rest. If something's broken, or you
      just want to say hi, I'm at <a href="https://mm.coffee" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">mm.coffee</a>.
    </p>
  </SitePage>
</Base>
